          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A
            %em bi-brane
            is to a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QFT+with+defects"} defect
            in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FQFT"} FQFT
            as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/brane"} brane
            is to a boundary condition.
          %p The term “bi-brane” was apparently introduced in
          %ul
            %li
              Jürgen Fuchs, Christoph Schweigert, Konrad Waldorf,
              %em Bi-branes: Target Space Geometry for World Sheet topological Defects
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/0703145"}> arXiv
              )
          %p The description below approaches the concept in a slightly more abstract context.
          %p
            The notion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/brane"} brane
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bi-brane"} bi-brane
            can be made very abstract, but to get the main idea it is useful to start with considering what is usually called the
            = succeed "." do
              %em geometric case
          %p Recall for instance from
          %ul
            %li
              Jacek Brodzki, Varghese Mathai, Jonathan Rosenberg, Richard J. Szabo,
              %em D-Branes, RR-Fields and Duality on Noncommutative Manifolds
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/0607020"}> arXiv
              ,
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://golem.ph.utexas.edu/string/archives/000879.html"} blog
          %p
            that a geometric
            %em brane
            on some space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            and a bundle gerbe
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒢
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{G}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                a map
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ι
                      %mo :
                      %mi Q
                      %mo →
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \iota : Q \to X
            %li
              %p
                a morphism
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi σ
                      %mo :
                      %mn 1
                      %mo →
                      %msup
                        %mi ι
                        %mo *
                      %mi 𝒢
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma : 1 \to \iota^* \mathcal{G}
                from the trivial 2-vector bundle on
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Q
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
                into the pullback of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒢
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{G}
                to
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Q
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
                – this morphism is called a
                %em gerbe module
                or
                = succeed "." do
                  %em twisted vector bundle
          %p
            If we write this more diagrammatically using the classifying (fiber-assigning) cocycle
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mn 2
                  %mi Vect
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g : X \to 2 Vect
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒢
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{G}
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi Q
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↙
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo ↘
                            %mi ι
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi pt
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo ⇒
                            %mi σ
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi X
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↘
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mo ↙
                            %mi g
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mn 2
                          %mi Vect
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \&amp;&amp; Q
                  \\\
                  \&amp; \swarrow &amp;&amp; \searrow^{\iota}
                  \\\
                  pt &amp;&amp;\stackrel{\sigma}{\Rightarrow}&amp;&amp; X
                  \\\
                  \&amp; \searrow &amp;&amp; \swarrow_{g}
                  \\\
                  \&amp;&amp; 2 Vect
                  }
                  \\,.
          %p Conceived in this form the notion has an obvious generalizations:
          %p
            let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            be two possibly different spaces with two possibly different 2-vector bundles on them, classified by cocycles
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi g
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g_1
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi g
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g_2
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                a span
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mover
                          %mo ←
                          %mrow
                            %msub
                              %mi ι
                              %mn 1
                        %mi Q
                        %mover
                          %mo →
                          %mrow
                            %msub
                              %mi ι
                              %mn 2
                        %mi Y
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                        x \stackrel{\iota_1}{\leftarrow} Q
                        \\stackrel{\iota_2}{\rightarrow} Y
            %li
              %p and a transformation between the two pulled back bundles
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi Q
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mrow
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi ι
                                %mn 1
                          %mo ↙
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo ↘
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi ι
                                %mn 2
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi X
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo ⇒
                            %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} bibrane
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi Y
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mrow
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi g
                                %mn 1
                          %mo ↘
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mo ↙
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi g
                                %mn 2
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mn 2
                          %mi Vect
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \&amp;&amp; Q
                  \\\
                  \&amp; {}^{\iota_1}\swarrow &amp;&amp; \searrow^{\iota_2}
                  \\\
                  X &amp;&amp;\stackrel{\bibrane}{\Rightarrow}&amp;&amp; Y
                  \\\
                  \&amp; {}_{g_1}\searrow &amp;&amp;&amp; \swarrow_{g_2}
                  \\\
                  \&amp;&amp; 2 Vect
                  }
                  \\,.
          %p The description of branes in the above diagrammatic form was first given in
          %ul
            %li
              Urs Schreiber,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/quant2states.pdf", :title => "Quantum 2-states: sections of 2-vector bundles"} Quantum 2-states: sections of 2-vector bundles
              %em Higher categories and their applications
              (2007)
          %p and described in more detail in
          %ul
            %li
              Urs Schreiber, Konrad Waldorf,
              %em Connections on Nonabelian Gerbes and their Holonomy
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0808.1923"} arXiv
              = succeed ")." do
                %a{:href => "http://golem.ph.utexas.edu/category/2008/08/connections_on_nonabelian_gerb.html"} blog
          %p The generalization to bi-branes is developed at
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/Nonabelian+cocycles+and+their+quantum+symmetries"}> Nonabelian cocycles and their quantum symmetries
              \.
          %p This is very closely related to the spans appearing in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+function+theory"}> geometric function theory
              \.
          %p
            The relation is discussed a bit at
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "http://golem.ph.utexas.edu/category/2009/01/benzvi_on_geometric_function_t.html#c021321"} this blog entry
          %p
            At least some aspects of the concept have more or less implicitly been considered before, notably in the context of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+T-duality"} topological T-duality
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "http://golem.ph.utexas.edu/category/2007/02/qft_of_charged_nparticle_tdual.html"} here
          %p The interpretation of T-duality in terms of bi-branes is discussed in more detail in
          %ul
            %li
              Gor Sarkissian, Christoph Schweigert,
              %em Some remarks on defects and T-duality
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0810.3159"}> arXiv
              )
          %h2#bibranes_motivated_from_2d_cft Bi-branes motivated from 2d CFT
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-spectral+triple"} Recall
            that from a 2-dimensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conformal+field+theory"} CFT
            one induces a (generalized)
            %em target space
            geometry in generalization of how a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectral+triple"} spectral triple
            induces such a generalized geometry.
          %p
            From category-algebraic considerations one obtains
            = succeed "s" do
              %span.newWikiWord
                defect line
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/defect+line"} ?
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conformal+field+theory"} CFT
            %strong Bi-branes
            are the answer to the question: “What is the target space structure corresponding to defect lines in the 2d CFT”?
          %p For certain 2-d CFTs based on current algebras the bi-branes corresponding to certain defect lines in these theories have been introduced and discussed in
          %ul
            %li
              Jürgen Fuchs, Christoph Schweigert, Konrad Waldorf,
              %em Bi-branes: Target Space Geometry for World Sheet topological Defects
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/0703145"}> arXiv
              )
          %h2#in_wzw_theories In WZW theories
          %p
            In the above article it is found that, just as symmetric conformal branes in WZW models, whose target space is a Lie group
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            given by conjugacy classes in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/span"} span
            %em correspondences
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi B
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↙
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↘
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi G
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \&amp;&amp; B
                  \\\
                  \&amp; \swarrow &amp;&amp;\searrow
                  \\\
                  G &amp;&amp;&amp;&amp; G
                  }
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+group+character"} biconjugacy class
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
